   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p18-information-block",
     "replacement": "If $f$ is invertible, you can solve an equation such as $f^{-1}(x)=k$ by applying $f$ to both sides.\n\nSince $f\\bigl(f^{-1}(x)\\bigr)=x$, this gives $x=f(k)$ without finding $f^{-1}(x)$.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p18-information-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "4dccb4b31e8281032c0b1e0089c37dd0c449383e9db6937f7e3de717e2f7dc16",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/inverse-functions-2-v1/20261111-024628",
   "revision": 12
  },
  "23d9f92c-a82e-4544-b323-9c05914731dd": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p26-restricting-domain",
     "replacement": "If a function's inverse is not a function, restrict the domain so that the restricted function has an inverse that is a function.\n\n- A function is **invertible** if its inverse is also a function.\n- A function is invertible exactly when it is **one-to-one**: each output comes from exactly one input.\n- To make a function invertible, restrict its domain using its **turning points**.\n- Restrict the domain so that the curve is **strictly increasing** or **strictly decreasing**. To keep the domain as large as possible, include the turning point as an endpoint.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p26-restricting-domain",
     "targetFingerprint": "23c78ace6da302115e4079183123af8557d8d7c4508eb99e537f7fad5f324206",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/inverse-functions-2-v1/20261111-024628",
   "revision": 12
  },
  "16e82648-ce69-4bf3-ba77-0ccf61f251ce": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p27-teaching",
     "replacement": "To find the domain of the inverse of a function with a restricted domain, use one of these methods:\n\n- Use the range of the original function as the domain of the inverse.\n- Sketch the reflection of the original function in $y=x$.\n- After swapping $x$ and $y$, apply the restriction to $y$ and solve the inequality for $x$.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p27-teaching",
     "targetFingerprint": "354f8266b24e12b529768f1ce12f081de56e76dc40a919e85f566e344c864bb6",
